  "query_preview": "(Wednesday through Sunday):\n\nMain forecast concerns for this cycle are in the long term, \nregarding rain chances mid week and temperatures late week.\n\nMorning synoptic models continue to develop a shortwave trough\nover the Pacific Northwest Monday night, deepening over the\ncentral Plains Tuesday night. Getting past Wednesday, additional\nwaves will drop southward out of Ontario, resulting in a \nsprawling upper low dominating the eastern CONUS by Friday. This\nwill provide the region with significantly cooler air, with \nLREF guidance highlighting a 40-50% chance of highs below 45 \ndegrees near/north of I-74 Thursday, and 60-80% chance in most \nareas north of I-70 Friday. NBM temperatures toward the end of \nthe period (next weekend) were lowered due to biases near the \nGreat Lakes, though the morning GFS ensembles suggest some \ntemperature recovery by Sunday just to our northwest.\n\nRegarding precipitation trends, chances continue to increase\nWednesday (now about 60% near/north of I-74) as the shortwave\ndigs over Iowa/Missouri. Much of this will fall during the\nafternoon. A few hundred joules of CAPE may be enough to trigger\na few rumbles of thunder as well. With this storm system\nintensifying in the general vicinity on Thursday, some lingering\nshowers are most likely over eastern Illinois. Getting into\nThursday night, surface flow becomes more oriented off Lake\nMichigan, with a mixture of rain and snow showers downstream  \nover northeast Illinois and northwest Indiana. Depending on the \nultimate wind orientation off the lake, a few of these may sneak\ninto east central Illinois near Danville late Thursday night or\nearly Friday morning. This threat should become more focused\nover Indiana later Friday, as a ridge of high pressure edges\ninto the mid-Mississippi Valley.",
